\details{
An ordinal family (\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()}, \code{acat()})
takes the response's level order as the category order. Supply an
ordered factor, or integer codes \verb{1..K}. An unordered factor is
refused, as brms refuses it, because its level order is alphabetical
unless someone set it, and that order is the model.

A \code{bernoulli()} response can hold any two values, which are coded 0
and 1 by level order as brms codes them: the levels of a factor, the
sorted values of a number or a character vector (so \code{-2} and \code{-1}
become 0 and 1, and so do \code{1} and \code{2}), and \code{FALSE} and \code{TRUE} of a
logical. A number with one value is the 1 of 0 and 1 unless the value
is 0. The fit keeps the coding, and codes newdata's response with it.
A third value is refused with brms's message. Two values that both
lie strictly between 0 and 1, such as 0.1 and 0.9, are coded too, as
brms codes them, with a warning: they are usually proportions, which
\code{binomial()} with \code{trials()} or \code{Beta()} fits. \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} and the
draws return the codes 0 and 1, as brms's \code{posterior_predict()} does.

A response with only two outcomes gets brms's message suggesting
\code{bernoulli()}: an ordinal or categorical response with two
categories, and a \code{binomial()}, \code{beta_binomial()} or
\code{zero_inflated_binomial()} response whose trials are all one.

Every constructor has brms's fields: \verb{$link} is the name of the link
for the mean, and \verb{$link_<dpar>} the link of each other parameter,
as in \code{beta_binomial()$link_phi}. See \code{\link[=frmtmb_family]{frmtmb_family()}}.
}
\section{Compositional responses}{

\code{dirichlet()} and \code{logistic_normal()} model a row of proportions that
sums to one, brms's simplex families. Write the response as an
\verb{n x K} matrix: \code{cbind(y1, y2, y3)}, or a matrix column of the data
(\code{dd$Y <- Y}). Every row must sum to one, to within 1.5e-8, and every
proportion must lie strictly between 0 and 1. The column names are
the categories, and the FIRST is the reference category unless
\code{refcat} names another, as in brms.

Each non-reference category \code{k} gets its own linear predictor
\verb{mu<k>}, and the main formula applies to every one of them;
\code{bf(Y ~ x, muc ~ z)} gives category \code{c} a predictor of its own. A
group term \code{(1 | g)} gives each category its own effects, and
\code{(1 | ID | g)} correlates them across categories, as for
\code{categorical()}.

\code{dirichlet()} is \code{y ~ Dirichlet(phi * softmax(mu))}: the category
means are the softmax of the predictors, with the reference
category's held at zero, and \code{phi} is the precision, which
\code{bf(phi ~ z)} models. \code{refcat = NA} predicts every category, which
brms allows; without a prior the predictors are then identified
only up to a common shift, and the fit says so.

\code{logistic_normal()} makes the log ratios against the reference
category, \code{log(y_k / y_ref)}, multivariate normal with means
\verb{mu<k>}, standard deviations \verb{sigma<k>} and correlations \code{lncor}.
Each \verb{sigma<k>} takes its own formula; \code{lncor} is one correlation
matrix for the model, reported as \code{lncor(b,c)} in the summary, and
takes an \code{lkj()} prior under \code{class = "lncor"}.

\code{fitted()} on a \code{dirichlet()} fit returns brms's \verb{n x 4 x K} array of
category means, layers named \code{P(Y = k)}. A logistic-normal mean has
no closed form, so \code{fitted()} and \code{conditional_effects()} are refused
on it, as in brms. On both, \code{predict()} and \code{simulate()} draw
compositions (rows on the simplex), \code{frm_linpred(dpar = "mub")} gives
one category's predictor, and \code{residuals()} is refused, as brms
refuses it for every family whose response is a set of categories.

brms also has \code{dirichlet2}, not exported there, reached as
\code{brmsfamily("dirichlet2")}: one predictor per category on the scale
of its Dirichlet concentration (log link), and no precision. The
same spelling reaches it here.
}

\section{The negative binomial in sigma}{

\code{brmsfamily("negbinomial2")} is brms's \code{negbinomial2}: the negative
binomial of \code{negbinomial()} with the dispersion \code{sigma = 1 / shape},
so the variance is \code{mu + sigma * mu^2}, and \code{sigma} near 0 is the
Poisson. brms does not export a constructor for it, and neither does
frmtmb. \code{sigma} takes the positive links (\code{link_sigma}, default
\code{"log"}) and its own formula, \code{bf(y ~ x, sigma ~ z)}. The family is
\code{negbinomial()} underneath: the same density, simulator, mean and
variance, and the same addition terms, so \code{rate(d)} multiplies the
mean and the shape \code{1 / sigma} by \code{d}, as in brms. The fit is
\code{negbinomial()}'s with \code{sigma} in place of \code{1 / shape}: the
log-likelihood is the same and only the parameter is reported
differently.
}

\section{Hurdle and zero-one-inflated responses}{

A hurdle family gives every zero to \code{hu}, the hurdle probability, and
truncates its count or continuous part at zero. For
\code{hurdle_negbinomial()}, \code{P(Y = 0) = hu} and a positive count \code{y} has
probability \verb{(1 - hu) NB(y | mu, shape) / (1 - NB(0 | mu, shape))},
so \code{mu} is the mean of the untruncated negative binomial and not the
mean of the positive counts.

\code{zero_one_inflated_beta()} takes a response in \verb{[0, 1]}. \code{zoi} is the
probability of an exact 0 or 1, \code{coi} is the probability that such a
value is 1, and a value strictly between 0 and 1 follows a beta
distribution with mean \code{mu} and precision \code{phi}. The expected
response, which \code{fitted()} returns, is \code{zoi * coi + (1 - zoi) * mu}.
\code{coi} is scored only on the values at exactly 0 or 1. A response with
0s but no 1 (or the reverse) sends \code{coi} to that boundary, and one
with neither gives \code{coi} no data at all, so that no standard error of
the fit is usable; the fit warns in both cases. brms fits such data
through its prior on \code{coi}. Here, hold \code{coi} at a value with
\code{bf(coi = 0.5)}.

\code{zero_inflated_beta_binomial()} is \code{beta_binomial()} with a
zero-inflation probability \code{zi}: \verb{P(Y = 0) = zi + (1 - zi) BB(0)},
and a count \code{y > 0} has probability \verb{(1 - zi) BB(y)}, with the trials
from \code{trials()}. \code{fitted()} returns \code{(1 - zi) * mu * trials}.

\code{hurdle_cumulative()} is brms's hurdle over an ordinal response. The
response is \verb{0..K}: 0 is the hurdle, with probability \code{hu}, and the
categories \verb{1..K} follow \code{cumulative()} with probabilities scaled by
\code{1 - hu}. An ordered factor takes its FIRST level as the category 0.
\code{fitted()} returns the \code{K + 1} category probabilities, 0 first. The
expected category that \code{conditional_effects()} shows by default is
scored by these codes, so the hurdle scores 0; brms scores each
column by its position and reads one higher. \code{disc} and \code{threshold}
work as for the other ordinal families (see Ordinal thresholds and
discrimination). \code{thres(x = )} and \code{thres(gr = )} count the
categories above the hurdle, and \code{thres(gr = )} keeps the hurdle in
each group's density, as brms's \verb{hurdle_cumulative_*_merged_lpmf}
does. \code{cs()} takes the category-specific offsets off each row's
thresholds, as in brms; they can make two thresholds cross for some
rows, and the probability of the category between them is then
negative. The density is then \code{NaN}, as brms's is, and so are the
row's \code{fitted()} probabilities, where brms returns the negative
difference; \code{simulate()} gives \code{NA} for the row. \code{cs()} and
\code{thres(gr = )} together are refused, as in brms.
}

\section{Ordinal thresholds and discrimination}{

Every ordinal family (\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()},
\code{acat()} and \code{hurdle_cumulative()}) has brms's discrimination
parameter \code{disc}. The distribution function is read at
\code{disc * (tau_k - eta)} (at \code{disc * (eta - tau_k)} for \code{cratio()} and
\code{acat()}), with \code{disc} held at 1 unless the formula models it, as in
brms: \code{bf(y ~ x, disc ~ z)}. Its link is \code{link_disc}, \code{"log"} by
default. The likelihood cannot tell an intercept in \code{disc} apart from
the scale of the thresholds, so write \code{disc ~ 0 + z}. brms accepts
\code{disc ~ 1 + z} and holds the intercept with its default
\code{normal(0, 1)} prior; frmtmb fits it too, and warns unless a prior
holds the intercept, as it does for cell-means columns that add up to
one (\code{disc ~ 0 + h}). Held at 1, \code{disc} shows nowhere in the output,
as in brms: not on the \verb{Links:} line, not as a fixed parameter, and
not in \code{fixef(flatten = TRUE)} or \code{coef()}.

\code{threshold} selects brms's threshold structure. \code{"flexible"}
estimates each threshold. \code{"equidistant"} estimates the first
threshold and the distance \code{delta} between neighbors, so that
\code{tau_k = tau_1 + (k - 1) * delta}; \code{variables()} and \code{summary()} name
it \code{delta} as brms does, and \code{set_prior(class = "delta")} gives it a
prior, while class \code{"Intercept"} addresses the first threshold only.
\code{delta} is positive for \code{cumulative()} and \code{hurdle_cumulative()},
whose thresholds are ordered. \code{"sum_to_zero"} holds the thresholds
to a sum of zero and does not center the design, as in brms. brms
declares one parameter per threshold and relies on its prior for the
common location, which the likelihood does not see; frmtmb estimates
the \code{K - 2} free directions instead, so class \code{"Intercept"} has
nothing to address there and is refused. \code{thres(gr = )} gives each
level its own first threshold and \code{delta}, or its own zero sum.
\code{"equidistant"} needs two thresholds per vector, since \code{delta} has
nothing to measure on one. \code{"sum_to_zero"} holds a single threshold
at zero, a vector with no parameter, which brms runs under
\code{thres(gr = )} and frmtmb also fits without it.
}

\section{Extended-support beta}{

\code{xbeta()} is the extended-support beta of Kosmidis and Zeileis
(2024), brms's \code{xbeta}, for a response in \verb{[0, 1]} with exact 0s and
1s. A latent beta variable with mean \code{mu} and precision \code{phi} is
stretched by the exceedance \code{kappa} to \verb{(1 + 2 kappa) Z - kappa} and
censored at 0 and 1, so the ends carry the latent mass beyond them.
\code{mu} is the mean of the latent variable, not of the response;
\code{fitted()} returns the mean of the response, brms's
\code{posterior_epred()}. The mass at each end is an incomplete beta
function, which is taken mostly from a continued fraction rather
than from \code{RTMB::pbeta()}, whose third derivatives are not finite
everywhere and which therefore failed the Laplace approximation of a
model with random effects.

\code{kappa} is placed mostly by the rows at exactly 0 or 1. With none,
only the shape of the interior places it, and it can run to 0,
where the model is \code{Beta()}, or grow without bound. When it runs to
0, or a coefficient of a log-link \code{kappa} has a standard
error above 10 or none, the fit warns; brms fits such data through its
\code{gamma(0.01, 0.01)} prior on \code{kappa}. Compare the fit with \code{Beta()},
or hold \code{kappa} at a value with \code{bf(kappa = )}.
}

\section{Categorical (nominal) responses}{

\code{categorical()} fits a multinomial logit to an unordered factor. One
category is the reference: its linear predictor is held at zero, and
each other category gets its own predictor named \verb{mu<Level>}, as in
brms. The reference is the FIRST level, or the level that \code{refcat}
names: \code{categorical(refcat = "b")} gives the dpars \code{mua} and \code{muc}.
Changing the reference changes the coefficients, not the model: the
log-likelihood and the category probabilities stay the same. The main
model formula applies to every predictor, and any single category is
overridden by naming it: \code{bf(y ~ x, muc ~ z)} gives category \code{"c"}
its own predictor and leaves the rest on \code{~ x}. A dpar name is
brms's: dots and underscores in a level are dropped, so the level
\code{"p.q"} gives \code{mupq}.

\code{refcat = NA} gives every category a predictor, as brms allows. The
likelihood then stays the same when one number is added to every
predictor, so a maximum-likelihood fit cannot determine the
coefficients, only the category probabilities, and the fit warns
that their standard errors are not available. A prior on each
predictor (\code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}) determines them. brms leans on its priors
here too, and with its default priors (flat on the slopes) its
sampler does not converge on this model.

The categories come from the data, so \code{frm()} reads them off the
response before it parses the formula. Constructing the family away
from a data set (to inspect it, or to reach the parser through
another entry point) needs them stated: \code{categorical(levels = c("a", "b", "c"))}, or \code{categorical(K = 3)} for a response already coded
\verb{1..K}, which names the dpars \verb{mu2 ... muK}. A character or logical
response is coerced to a factor with a message naming the level
order, because that order is the model.

\code{fitted()} returns brms's \verb{n x 4 x K} array of category
probabilities, one layer per category named \verb{P(Y = <level>)}, and
\code{frm_linpred(type = "response")} the \verb{n x K} matrix of the estimates,
rows summing to one: the convention the ordinal families follow.
\code{fitted(scale = "linear")} stacks the predictors into an
\verb{n x 4 x (K - 1)} array, layers \verb{eta1, eta2, ...} (\code{K} layers under
\code{refcat = NA}), as brms does; with one predictor (two categories and
a reference) it stays an \verb{n x 4} matrix, as in brms. The intervals of
\code{fitted()} here and for \code{multinomial()} are Wald intervals, the
estimate plus or minus a normal quantile times its delta-method
standard error, held inside the range of the quantity (0 to 1 for a
probability, 0 to the trials for a count), so near the edge of that
range a bound can be exactly 0. \code{frm_linpred(dpar = "mub")} gives one
category's predictor, which is where \code{se.fit} lives. \code{simulate()}
draws factor levels.

\code{multinomial()} is the same likelihood on an \verb{n x K} matrix of counts,
with the number of trials in \code{trials()}, as in brms. The columns are
the categories: \code{cbind(y1, y2, y3)} gives the dpars \code{muy2} and \code{muy3},
and an unnamed matrix numbers them \code{mu2}, \code{mu3}. \code{refcat} works as
for \code{categorical()}. \code{fitted()} returns brms's \verb{n x 4 x K} array of
expected COUNTS, probability times trials, which brms labels
\verb{P(Y = <category>)} too. \code{multinomial(K = 3)} builds the family away
from the data, with the categories \verb{1..K}. A one-hot matrix with one
trial a row gives the log-likelihood of \code{categorical()}.

\code{dirichlet_multinomial()} is brms's overdispersed multinomial. The
category probabilities of each row are Dirichlet with mean
\code{softmax(mu)} and precision \code{phi}, and the counts are multinomial
given them. The expected counts are those of \code{multinomial()}, and
the covariance of a row is the multinomial one times
\code{(T + phi) / (1 + phi)} at \code{T} trials, so a large \code{phi} is close to
\code{multinomial()}. \code{phi} takes a log link and its own formula,
\code{bf(Y | trials(n) ~ x, phi ~ z)}. Everything else is as for
\code{multinomial()}: the response matrix, \code{trials()}, \code{refcat},
\code{fitted()}, \code{K} and \code{simulate()}, which draws the probabilities of
each row from the Dirichlet and then the counts. With two categories
it is \code{beta_binomial()} with \code{phi} the same precision.
}

\section{Circular responses}{

\code{von_mises()} models an angle in radians on \verb{(-pi, pi]}. Its \code{mu} is
the mean direction and takes the \code{tan_half} link, which maps the
whole line onto that interval; \code{kappa} is the concentration and takes
a log link, with \code{kappa = 0} the uniform distribution on the circle.
Both are brms's choices. \code{fitted()} and \code{frm_linpred(type = "response")}
report the mean direction. The normalizing constant needs
\verb{log I0(kappa)}, which RTMB differentiates exactly through its own
\code{besselI} method, so nothing here is a series approximation.
Residuals are differences of angles and are NOT wrapped, so read
\code{residuals()} on a von Mises fit with that in mind.
}

\section{Quantile regression inference}{

\code{asym_laplace()} and \code{zero_inflated_asym_laplace()} fit quantile
regression through a WORKING likelihood: at a fixed \code{quantile} the
point estimates are consistent quantile estimates (they match
\code{quantreg::rq()}), but the asymmetric Laplace is not the data's
true density, so Wald standard errors and \code{confint()} intervals
computed from it are not calibrated. This is a property of the
asymmetric-Laplace approach, shared by brms. Use
\code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} for intervals you can defend. The check
function's kink can also produce a benign false-convergence
warning near the optimum; \code{frm_allfit()} confirms the fit when in
doubt.
}

\section{Degrees of freedom that run off}{

\code{student()} estimates \code{nu} on the \code{logm1} link, which holds it above
one but puts no ceiling on it. A student-t reaches \code{gaussian()} only
in the limit \code{nu -> Inf}, so on data with no heavy tails the
likelihood keeps rising as \code{nu} grows and the maximum is never
attained. The fit then reports whatever \code{nu} the optimizer last
reached, with a standard error to match: values of \code{1e9} and above
are ordinary, and two runs of the same model on the same data in a
different row order can differ by orders of magnitude in \code{nu} while
agreeing on every coefficient to the last bit.

That is the model saying the data shows no heavy tails, not a
failure to converge. \code{\link[=diagnose]{diagnose()}} names it under "Distributional
parameter at the end of its link". Read the fit as the gaussian one
it has become. If you want a number you can report, refit with
\code{gaussian()}, or hold \code{nu} somewhere finite with a prior (see
\code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}); brms does the same with its default
\code{gamma(2, 0.1)}.

\code{nu} runs off the OTHER end too, and there the reading is opposite.
On tails heavier than any identified \code{nu} can hold, Cauchy data for
instance, \code{nu} goes down to one instead: \code{log(nu - 1)} around -20,
a standard error in the thousands, and a natural-scale value that
prints as \code{1}. \code{\link[=diagnose]{diagnose()}} names that under the same heading. The
remedy is not \code{gaussian()}, which is the worst fit available for
such data. There the data is the message; a prior is still the way
to hold \code{nu} finite.

The likelihood itself stays accurate the whole way. The log density
is formed so that \verb{log Gamma((nu + 1) / 2) - log Gamma(nu / 2)}
never cancels, which holds it to 7e-15 of a 300-bit reference for
every \code{nu} up to \code{1e50}.
}

\section{Robust regression}{

\code{huber()} fits Huber's least-favorable distribution: gaussian within
\code{k} residual standard deviations of \code{mu} and Laplace outside, so a
far-out point pulls on the fit with a bounded influence instead of
its squared distance. It is a proper normalized density, not a
penalty, so this is ordinary maximum likelihood and \code{logLik()},
\code{AIC()} and the likelihood-ratio machinery all mean what they say.

\code{k} is a fixed constant of the family, \code{huber(k = 1.345)}, not a
distributional parameter. It states where the analyst draws the line
between a residual and an outlier, which is a modelling choice rather
than something the data identifies; \code{MASS::rlm()} treats it the same
way. Estimating it would let the likelihood buy fit by widening the
gaussian core, which is the opposite of the point. As \code{k} grows the
family collapses to \code{gaussian()}.

Point estimates track \code{MASS::rlm(psi = psi.huber)} closely but not
exactly, and the difference is the scale: \code{rlm()} fixes the scale at
a MAD-type estimate and iterates the location, while \code{huber()}
estimates \code{sigma} by maximum likelihood jointly with \code{mu}. The
coefficients agree to about \code{1e-2} on well-behaved data; the
\code{sigma} estimates need not.

The working-likelihood caveat that applies to \code{asym_laplace()}
applies here for the same reason. If the data are not actually
Huber-distributed - and the family is chosen precisely because the
error distribution is unknown - then the model is misspecified, the
information matrix is not the variance of the score, and Wald
standard errors and \code{confint()} intervals from it are not
calibrated. The point estimates stay consistent for the location.
Use \code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} for intervals you can defend.
\code{cens()} and \code{trunc()} are unavailable: the CDF is a three-piece
function of a parameter-dependent residual and has no branch-free
form for the tape.

\code{rho} has a kink at \verb{|u| = k}, so the objective is only piecewise
smooth and the optimizer often stops with a maximum absolute
gradient around \code{1e-4} and the accompanying false-convergence
warning. That is the kink, not a bad fit: the same thing happens to
\code{asym_laplace()}. The estimates satisfy Huber's own estimating
equations, \verb{X' psi(u) = 0} with \code{psi(u) = min(max(u, -k), k)}, to
the same order. \code{\link[=frm_allfit]{frm_allfit()}} confirms the fit when in doubt.
}

\section{Conway-Maxwell-Poisson counts}{

In \code{compois()}, \code{mu} is the mean of the count and \code{nu} is the
dispersion: \code{nu > 1} gives less variance than the Poisson, and
\code{nu < 1} more. The density is \code{RTMB::dcompois2()}, so
\code{exp(Intercept)} under the log link is the expected count. This is
not brms's parameterization. brms 2.23.0's \code{com_poisson()} fits \code{mu}
as the rate \code{lambda} of the density \verb{lambda^y / (y!)^nu}, which is
not the mean unless \code{nu = 1}. On counts simulated with \code{lambda = 9}
and \code{nu = 2} (n = 400), frmtmb's \code{exp(Intercept)} is 2.78, the sample
mean, and brms's is 7.30. The coefficients of the two fits are not
comparable one by one (\code{dev/remeasure-findings.md}, R6).
}

\section{Links}{

Every constructor takes \code{link} for the mean and a \verb{link_<dpar>} for
each of its other distributional parameters, following brms:
\code{student(link_sigma = "softplus")}, \code{zero_inflated_poisson(link_zi = "identity")}. \link{frmtmb-links} lists the whole roster, what each link
maps, which families take it for the mean, and which set each
parameter admits.

The four families 'stats' owns, \code{gaussian()}, \code{poisson()},
\code{binomial()} and \code{Gamma()}, have no frmtmb constructor to carry
these. Reach their links through \code{\link[=brmsfamily]{brmsfamily()}}:
\code{brmsfamily("gaussian", link_sigma = "softplus")}.

The link for the mean may be unquoted, \code{student(identity)}, and is
refused by name when brms does not allow it for the family:
\code{bernoulli("sqrt")} is an error.

An ordinal family's \code{link} is not a link on a mean. It names the
distribution function the thresholds are read through, so
all five ordinal families take \code{logit}, \code{probit}, \code{probit_approx},
\code{cloglog} and \code{cauchit} (and \code{cumulative()}, \code{hurdle_cumulative()}
and \code{acat()} also take \code{softit}), and refuse anything else. Off the
logit, \code{acat()} reads its categories in brms's second form, a product
of distribution functions times a reversed product of survivals (see
\link{frmtmb-links}).

\code{cumulative()} and \code{hurdle_cumulative()} keep their thresholds
increasing, because their category probabilities are differences of
the distribution function. A \code{cs()} term moves each threshold of a
row by its own amount, so it can make one row's thresholds cross;
brms fits that with a warning, and so does this package: such a
row's density and \code{fitted()} probabilities are \code{NaN}, and
\code{simulate()} gives \code{NA} there. The
thresholds of \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()} and \code{acat()} are unconstrained,
as in brms: their category probabilities are positive for any
thresholds, so a fit may have two of them cross.
}

\examples{
set.seed(4)
n <- 120
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(n))

# heavier tails than gaussian(), with an estimated df
dd$y <- 1 + 0.8 * dd$x + rt(n, df = 4)
fixef(frm(bf(y ~ x) + student(), data = dd))

# counts with more spread than poisson() allows
dd$cnt <- rnbinom(n, mu = exp(0.5 + 0.4 * dd$x), size = 2)
fit <- frm(bf(cnt ~ x) + negbinomial(), data = dd)
fixef_by_dpar(fit)$mu

# a zero-inflated count: the zi dpar gets its own predictor
dd$zi <- ifelse(runif(n) < 0.3, 0, dd$cnt)
frm(bf(zi ~ x, zi ~ 1) + zero_inflated_poisson(), data = dd)

# an ordered response: level order is the category order
dd$grade <- cut(1 + 0.8 * dd$x + rlogis(n), 3,
                labels = c("low", "mid", "high"), ordered_result = TRUE)
frm(bf(grade ~ x) + cumulative(), data = dd)

# a proportion in (0, 1)
dd$p <- plogis(0.2 + 0.6 * dd$x + rnorm(n, 0, 0.3))
frm(bf(p ~ x) + Beta(), data = dd)

# bounded influence: a few wild points barely move the slope
dd$rob <- 1 + 0.8 * dd$x + rnorm(n)
dd$rob[1:5] <- dd$rob[1:5] + 30
fixef_by_dpar(frm(bf(rob ~ x), family = huber(), data = dd))$mu
fixef_by_dpar(frm(bf(rob ~ x), family = gaussian(), data = dd))$mu

# an unordered factor: one predictor per non-reference category,
# named after the level it belongs to
dd$pick <- factor(sample(c("ale", "stout", "lager"), n, TRUE))
cat_fit <- frm(bf(pick ~ x), family = categorical(), data = dd)
fixef(cat_fit)                     # mulager and mustout; ale is the
                                   # reference
head(fitted(cat_fit))              # n x K category probabilities

# one category may take its own predictor
dd$w <- rnorm(n)
frm(bf(pick ~ x, mustout ~ w), family = categorical(), data = dd)

# an angle: mu is the mean direction, kappa the concentration
dd$angle <- atan2(sin(0.5 + dd$x), cos(0.5 + dd$x))
vm_fit <- frm(bf(angle ~ x), family = von_mises(), data = dd)
head(fitted(vm_fit))               # the mean direction, in radians
}
